{"n":68,"paragraphs":["A poor man wanted money, and he had heard that a ghost could be made to fetch you anything you asked for. So he went searching for someone who could give him one, and finally found a sage with great powers. The sage asked why he wanted a ghost. To work for me, said the man. The sage told him to go home and stop bothering himself.","The man came back the next day, weeping and pleading. At last the sage, fed up, gave him a charm and a magic word. Repeat it, he said, and a ghost will appear and obey you. But be careful: these are fearsome creatures and must be kept busy every moment. Leave one idle and it will take your life. The man was sure he could find work for it forever.","In a forest he chanted the word until a huge ghost appeared, ready to serve, and warned that it would kill him the moment it had nothing to do. Build me a palace, said the man. Done. Bring me money. Here it is. Cut down this forest and build a city. Finished. Each job vanished in an instant, and the man grew scared. He had nothing left to give it.","The ghost threatened to eat him, so he fled to the sage and begged for his life. Just then the ghost arrived, about to swallow him. The sage told him to cut off the tail of a nearby curly-tailed dog and hand it to the ghost to straighten.","The ghost carefully straightened the tail, but the moment it let go, the tail curled right back. It tried again and again, for days on end, until it was worn out and confessed it had never been in such trouble in all its long career. It offered a deal: release me, keep everything I gave you, and I will never harm you. The man happily agreed.","Swamiji said the world is like that dog's tail. People have tried for centuries to straighten it, and it keeps curling back. Once we understand this, and learn to work without attachment, we stop becoming fanatics."],"learn":"Do good work, but don't make your peace depend on fixing the whole world exactly your way. When you let go of the demand for perfect results, you avoid burnout and the anger that turns people into fanatics, and the one who really gets straightened out is you.","source":"Vol. 1, pp. 77–79","images":["data:image/jpeg;base64,"]}